{"sentences": [{"sentence_id": "s1", "sentence": "Three Phase 2 trials of IL-17A inhibition in multiple sclerosis have been conducted, all by Novartis using secukinumab (AIN457).", "cites": ["NCT01874340"]}, {"sentence_id": "s2", "sentence": "One of the three trials was terminated early due to strategic development priorities, not safety concerns, with Novartis shifting to another anti-IL-17 antibody deemed to have better potential for MS.", "cites": ["NCT01874340"]}, {"sentence_id": "s3", "sentence": "Secukinumab has reached Phase 4 for psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, and ankylosing spondylitis, but not for multiple sclerosis.", "cites": ["CHEMBL1743068"]}, {"sentence_id": "s4", "sentence": "Ixekizumab, another IL-17A inhibitor, has also reached Phase 4 for psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis but has not advanced in multiple sclerosis.", "cites": ["CHEMBL1743034"]}, {"sentence_id": "s5", "sentence": "The FDA label for secukinumab includes no indication for multiple sclerosis and notes an increased risk of infections and candidiasis, with caution advised for inflammatory bowel disease.", "cites": ["FDA:77c4b13e-7df3-42d4-81db-3d0cddb7f67a"]}, {"sentence_id": "s6", "sentence": "A 2026 study in HLA-DR3 transgenic mice with EAE found that IL-17A is dispensable for brain-predominant disease in the absence of IFNγ, suggesting it may not be essential for all forms of neuroinflammation in MS models.", "cites": ["PMID:42876168"]}, {"sentence_id": "s7", "sentence": "Recent literature indicates Th17 cells and IL-17A are involved in MS pathogenesis, but their necessity is unclear, with studies showing disease can develop in their absence depending on immune context.", "cites": ["PMID:43011814"]}], "sources": [{"id": "NCT01874340", "text": "{"nct":"NCT01874340","title":"Efficacy and Safety of AIN457 (Secukinumab) in Patients With Relapsing Multiple Sclerosis","state":"stopped","drug":"ain457","start_year":2013,"end_year":2014,"phase":"2","overall_status":"TERMINATED","why_stopped":"Study terminated early based upon development of another anti-IL17 fully human monoclonal antibody with better potential for treating MS patients","lead_sponsor":"Novartis Pharmaceuticals"}
