{"counted":3,"counts_by_status":{"COMPLETED":2,"TERMINATED":1},"counts_by_phase":{"2":3},"total":3}"}, {"id": "CHEMBL1743068", "text": "{"chembl_id":"CHEMBL1743068","name":"SECUKINUMAB","max_phase":4.0,"mechanisms":[{"mechanism":"Interleukin 17A inhibitor","action":"INHIBITOR"}],"indications":[{"indication":"Arthritis, Psoriatic","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Immune System Diseases","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Psoriasis","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Spondylarthropathies","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Spondylitis, Ankylosing","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Arthritis, Rheumatoid","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Behcet Syndrome","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Giant Cell Arteritis","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Graves Ophthalmopathy","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Hidradenitis Suppurativa","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Lupus Nephritis","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Osteoarthritis","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Polymyalgia Rheumatica","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Tendinopathy","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Uveitis","max_phase":3.0}]}"}, {"id": "CHEMBL1743034", "text": "{"chembl_id":"CHEMBL1743034","name":"IXEKIZUMAB","max_phase":4.0,"mechanisms":[{"mechanism":"Interleukin 17A inhibitor","action":"INHIBITOR"}],"indications":[{"indication":"Arthritis, Psoriatic","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Immune System Diseases","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Psoriasis","max_phase":4.0},{"indication":"Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Spondylitis, Ankylosing","max_phase":3.0},{"indication":"Arthritis, Rheumatoid","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Constriction, Pathologic","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Depressive Disorder, Major","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Diabetes Mellitus, Type 1","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Guttate Psoriasis","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Pemphigoid, Bullous","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Pityriasis Rubra Pilaris","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Pyoderma","max_phase":2.0},{"indication":"Lichen Planus","max_phase":0.5}]}"}, {"id": "FDA:77c4b13e-7df3-42d4-81db-3d0cddb7f67a", "text": "{"set_id":"77c4b13e-7df3-42d4-81db-3d0cddb7f67a","boxed_warning":null,"warnings_and_cautions":"5 WARNINGS AND PRECAUTIONS Infections : Serious infections have occurred. Exercise caution when considering the use of COSENTYX in patients with a chronic infection or a history of recurrent infection. If a serious infection develops, discontinue COSENTYX until the infection resolves. ( 5.1 ) Hypersensitivity Reactions : If an anaphylactic reaction or other serious allergic reaction occurs, discontinue COSENTYX immediately and initiate appropriate therapy. ( 5.2 ) Tuberculosis (TB) : Prior to initiating treatment with COSENTYX, evaluate for TB. ( 5.3 ) Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) : Cases of IBD were observed in clinical trials. Exercise caution when prescribing COSENTYX to patients with IBD. ( 5.4 ) Eczematous Eruptions : Cases of severe eczematous eruptions have occurred in patients receiving COSENTYX. ( 5.5 ) Immunizations : Avoid use of live vaccines in patients treated with COSENTYX. ( 5.7 ) 5.1 Infections COSENTYX may increase the risk of infections. In clinical trials, a higher rate of infections was observed in COSENTYX treated subjects compared to placebo-treated subjects. In placebo-controlled clinical trials in subjects with moderate to severe PsO, higher rates of common infections, such as nasopharyngitis (11.4% versus 8.6%), upper respiratory tract infection (2.5% versus 0.7%) and mucocutaneous infections with candida (1.2% versus 0.3%) were observed in subjects treated with COSENTYX compared to placebo-treated subjects. A similar increase in risk of infecti…","indications_and_usage":"1 INDICATIONS AND USAGE COSENTYX is a human interleukin-17A antagonist indicated for the treatment of: moderate to severe plaque psoriasis (PsO) in adults and pediatric patients 6 years and older who are candidates for systemic therapy or phototherapy. ( 1.1 ) active psoriatic arthritis (PsA) in adults and pediatric patients 2 years of age and older. ( 1.2 ) active ankylosing spondylitis (AS) in adults and pediatric patients 12 years of age and older. ( 1.3 ) active non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis (nr-axSpA) in adults with objective signs of inflammation. ( 1.4 ) active enthesitis-related arthritis (ERA) in pediatric patients 4 years of age and older. ( 1.5 ) moderate to severe hidradenitis suppurativa (HS) in adults and pediatric patients 12 years of age and older. ( 1.6 ) 1.1 Moderate to Severe Plaque Psoriasis COSENTYX ® is indicated for the treatment of moderate to severe plaque psoriasis (PsO) in adults and pediatric patients 6 years and older who are candidates for systemic therapy or phototherapy. 1.2 Active Psoriatic Arthritis COSENTYX is indicated for the treatment of active psoriatic arthritis (PsA) in adults and pediatric patients 2 years of age and older. 1.3 Active Ankylosing Spondylitis COSENTYX is indicated for the treatment of active ankylosing spondylitis (AS) in adults and pediatric patients 12 years of age and older. 1.4 Active Non-Radiographic Axial Spondyloarthritis COSENTYX is indicated for the treatment of active non-radiographic axial spondyl…"}"}, {"id": "PMID:42876168", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42876168","title":"TNFα promotes brain-predominant EAE independent of IFNγ and IL-17A in HLA-DR3 transgenic mice.","year":"2026","journal":"Journal of immunology (Baltimore, Md. : 1950)","abstract":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is thought to be mediated by human leukocyte antigen (HLA) class II-restricted autoreactive CD4 T cells producing interleukin (IL)-17A and/or interferon γ (IFNγ), but the necessity of these cytokines for disease pathogenesis is unclear. Utilizing HLA-DR3 (DRβ1*0301) transgenic mice lacking IFNγ or IL-17A in experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis (EAE), a model of MS, we found that neither cytokine is essential for disease induction, although both have distinct roles. The absence of IFNγ caused atypical, brain-predominant EAE characterized by high levels of IL-17A, while the absence of IL-17A resulted in classical EAE, targeting the spinal cord. To assess whether IL-17A drives atypical EAE in the absence of IFNγ, we generated HLA-DR3.IFNγ-/-.IL-17A-/- double knockout (DKO) mice. DKO mice developed atypical EAE with brain infiltration and inflammation of the brainstem and cerebellum, indicating that IL-17A is dispensable for brain-predominant disease in the absence of IFNγ. DKO mice exhibited significant CD4 T cell infiltration and increased expression of CD4 T cell-associated genes at peak disease. Prior to central nervous system infiltration, CD4 T cell…"}
{"hit_count":7270}"}, {"id": "PMID:43011814", "text": "{"id":"PMID:43011814","title":"Th17 suppression strategies: targeting IL-17 and RORγt pathways in autoimmune diseases.","year":"2026","journal":"Frontiers in immunology","abstract":"T helper 17 (Th17) cells and their key effector cytokine, particularly IL-17A and IL-17F, play central roles in host defense against extracellular pathogens. However, they are also critically involved in the pathogenesis of many autoimmune and inflammatory diseases. Th17 cell differentiation, stabilization, plasticity, and pathogenicity are regulated by a network of cytokines and transcription factors, including IL-6, TGF-β, IL-23, STAT3, and the key transcription factor RORγt. RORγt-dependent pathways are also relevant to other immune populations, including group 3 innate lymphoid cells (ILC3s), highlighting the complexity of the IL-17 axis across tissues. Dysregulation of these pathways can lead to chronic inflammation and tissue damage in diseases such as psoriasis, rheumatoid arthritis, multiple sclerosis, and inflammatory bowel disease. Therapeutic targeting of the Th17 axis has led to clinically established approaches, particularly direct blockade of IL-17 signaling and inhibition of IL-23, whereas direct RORγt inhibition remains an investigational strategy. RORγt inhibitors may modulate Th17 differentiation and broader RORγt-dependent transcriptional programs, but their cli…"}
